A||N||16||beachbomb||Oil Leak!||||5||
Z||000000||beachbomb||10-27-2000||12:59 PM||||Hi all! I have a '84 Indy that has an oil leak coming from the passenger side of the engine. Thing is i cannot locate where it is coming from. I've looked every-where. I "think" it might be from the front engine oil seal? If this is it how hard is it to replace, any special tools? Please help. aNY suggestions will also help. It leaks just enough to leave a spot about 2 inches in diameter on the ground and only leaks from time to time, not all the time('kinda weird). Also i'm looking for Indy seats covers(anybody have any or know where i can get some), thanks alot..jim||||reg||5
Z||000001||William Federle||10-27-2000||01:36 PM||||I've got the (almost)exact same problem and I've got to find where it's coming from and fix it if<BR>possible this weekend. I'm hoping it's from the pan on that end. I noticed that the last two pan bolts aren't there and the holes are filled with silicone sealant.<P>I've had the car for slightly over a year (1984 SE manual transmission) and it just now became serious enough that I have to do something about it. It leaks on the belt which then squeals until the oil burns off.<P>If it turns out to be the seal it's time for another Fiero or another engine.<P>Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 10-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||beachbomb||10-27-2000||07:08 PM||||Sounds exactly like mine, i did put my car up on a lift to check it out, my oil pan is exactly like yours, two holes the have silicone coming thru them, i thought they where missing the screws but if you look real close(almost impossible to see), there are two bolts directly behind those silicone holes, so i'm guessing thats the way it is suppose to be since yours is that way as well. i checked all my pan bolts and they were tight. If you find out what your leak is coming from please e-mail me and let me know(hwsubman@bellsouth.net), thanks alot..jim||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||10-27-2000||07:34 PM||||I'll do that. I have to put a new starter in tomorrow and then when I'm able to start the car, I'll leave it up on jack-stands and pull the right rear wheel off to get a good look at that end of the engine while it's running. I'll let you know if I figure out where the leak is coming from.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||10-27-2000||08:02 PM||||My '85 4-cyl had a right-side engine oil leak when I bought it about 6 months ago.  Slimed the belt, left a parking puddle in front of the right rear wheel, sprayed oil on the right engine grille, etc.  The timing cover (crankshaft) seal was leaking.<P>Replacing the seal was relatively easy with the right rear wheel off and fender liner partly removed.  Since then, I have re-installed air conditioning, so it won't be as easy next time.<P>The leak recurred about three months later, I think because the crankshaft pulley hub is scored.  I polished the hub, and (knock on wood) it's still working.  If it leaks again, I guess I'll have to replace the hub or try to sleeve it.<P>Anyhow, it's probably not a terminal problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||beachbomb||10-27-2000||08:16 PM||||Thanks for the info, i'll take a look at that also. Did you have to pull the pulley off to get the timing belt cover off?||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||10-27-2000||08:57 PM||||Unless the Indy is different  (Can someone with Indy knowledge help us here?), you shouldn't need to remove the timing gear cover.  As I recall, you remove the AC and alternator belts, and the crankshaft pulley; then pry the seal out of the timing cover.  A seal puller helps.  Clean-up the seal recess, lube the seal lip, smear a little silicone sealant in the seal recess, and gently tap in the seal (lip pointing in).  Torque hell out of the crank pulley bolt.  (See specs.)<P>Do check the pulley hub for wear.  I polished mine with 600-grit paper and oil.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||beachbomb||10-28-2000||07:45 AM||||Thank alot for the info. I'll try it. The Indy's(to the best of my knowledge) have the exact engine as any other 4 cyclinder fireo's. Anyway, thanks alot for all you help, i'll luk if it works..jim||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||10-31-2000||08:16 PM||||How did you guys who did this already hold the crank imobile while you broke the bolt loose on the pully? I tried to just leave it in gear, but that isn't enough resistance to prevent the crank from turning. The haynes or chilton book says to take the starter off and bind the flywheel gear with a screwdriver but that sounds like a possible way to break a tooth.<P>How did you do it?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jake_Dragon||02-18-2001||04:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by William Federle:<BR><B>How did you guys who did this already hold the crank imobile while you broke the bolt loose on the pully? I tried to just leave it in gear, but that isn't enough resistance to prevent the crank from turning. The haynes or chilton book says to take the starter off and bind the flywheel gear with a screwdriver but that sounds like a possible way to break a tooth.<P>How did you do it?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok I know this is old but I have the same problem, how do you break this thing loose?<P>------------------<BR>Glenn Leber<BR>1985 2.5L Sport Coupe||||reg||4
Z||000010||rogergarrison||02-18-2001||05:24 PM||||Use an impact wrench on center bolt...but only to take it off, not put it back.||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrPBody||02-18-2001||06:20 PM||||I put the transmission in gear (5th), and applied the emergency brake.  Of course, this won't work with an automatic transmission.<P>Wedging the flywheel with a screwdriver probably will not hurt the ring gear teeth.  (My opinion.  Use your own judgement.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||02-18-2001||06:45 PM||||Just for the record:<P>I have NEVER broken a ring gear off anything in over 25 years by blocking the teeth with anything, screw driver, pry bar, use your imaagination. It ain't happened!||||reg||1
Z||000013||tgowens||02-18-2001||06:58 PM||||I use an air ratchet - works for me..........the thing to do though, when using a standard ratchet is to hold the pully with a pry bar between two of the bolt heads while you loosen another one - don't take them completely out until you have loosened them all.........<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||02-18-2001||07:27 PM||||Fel-Pro makes a sleeve kit for the balancer on the V-6's ($8 at Advance Auto), so I would think there is one for the 4's.  Mine was spitting oil out, too, and sleeving the balancer and installing a new seal finally fixed mine.  Under two hours from start to finish.||||reg||1
Z||000015||beachbomb||02-20-2001||04:36 PM||||Hey all, I used an air wrench, came off easy as pie. Oil leak was fixed in about 45 minutes time, problem was, looks like someone used a flat screw-driver instead of the proper gasket puller to pull of the front engine oil seal, i put new seal in and was done. no oil leak now. I did use air wrench to put back on, not sure if that was right or not but haven't had any problems with it..ttyl..Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||12
Z||000016||Jake_Dragon||02-20-2001||04:42 PM||||Could not get the large bolt out, did remove the pulley and found the remnants of an old v belt.<BR>The leak has all but gone away now that I don’t have the old belt going around the shaft.<BR>I plan on trading this tired old 2.5 for a shinny new one when the money is avalible.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jake_Dragon<BR>1985 2.5L Sport Coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/rebel33/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
